1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,289
How does the Army build a hospital in 72 hours?

2
00:00:04,289 --> 00:00:06,879
It brings the whole hospital with it.

3
00:00:07,329 --> 00:00:10,819
In November 2017, in an Army test, hospital units

4
00:00:10,819 --> 00:00:14,380
deployed from Fort Carson, Colorado, to Fort Polk,

5
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:15,093
Louisiana.

6
00:00:15,093 --> 00:00:18,796
Within 72 hours, they had hospital care with surgery

7
00:00:18,796 --> 00:00:21,218
and intensive care up and running.

8
00:00:21,668 --> 00:00:24,924
Tents, beds, operating rooms, intensive care, a lab,

9
00:00:24,924 --> 00:00:26,051
x-ray, a pharmacy.

10
00:00:26,051 --> 00:00:29,182
All of it packed to travel by truck and container.

11
00:00:29,632 --> 00:00:32,185
The idea is older than most people think.

12
00:00:32,185 --> 00:00:35,361
Its most famous version had a nickname you probably

13
00:00:35,361 --> 00:00:36,668
know from television.

14
00:00:37,068 --> 00:00:39,404
This is how a field hospital works.

15
00:00:39,404 --> 00:00:40,940
From the MASH to today.

16
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:45,734
Start with the Second World War, then Korea.

17
00:00:46,734 --> 00:00:50,391
In World War II, the Army ran evacuation hospitals

18
00:00:50,391 --> 00:00:52,512
in the theater of operations.

19
00:00:52,512 --> 00:00:56,022
A 1945 War Department film shows how one worked.

20
00:00:56,472 --> 00:00:59,151
Troops pitched the tents and built a water supply.

21
00:00:59,151 --> 00:01:01,508
Casualties came in through a receiving tent.

22
00:01:01,508 --> 00:01:03,918
Then patients moved on, by plane and train to

23
00:01:03,918 --> 00:01:06,704
general hospitals, and by ship to the United States.

24
00:01:07,154 --> 00:01:10,410
That idea, receive, treat, and move patients on, is

25
00:01:10,410 --> 00:01:12,645
still the core of a field hospital.

26
00:01:12,645 --> 00:01:15,646
In Korea, the Army relied on a smaller surgical

27
00:01:15,646 --> 00:01:17,945
hospital built to follow the troops.

28
00:01:18,395 --> 00:01:20,276
In the Korean War, the U.S.

29
00:01:20,276 --> 00:01:23,969
Army used Mobile Army Surgical Hospitals. MASH units.

30
00:01:23,969 --> 00:01:27,453
Small surgical hospitals that could follow an army

31
00:01:27,453 --> 00:01:28,359
in the field.

32
00:01:28,809 --> 00:01:32,985
This is real Army film of one of them, the 8209th

33
00:01:32,985 --> 00:01:37,075
MASH, shot by the Signal Corps in Korea in 1951.

34
00:01:37,525 --> 00:01:41,471
A year later, in August 1952, a photo caption placed

35
00:01:41,471 --> 00:01:45,265
the same unit about 20 miles from the front lines.

36
00:01:45,715 --> 00:01:48,796
In Korea, MASH units were first planned as 60-bed

37
00:01:48,796 --> 00:01:49,425
hospitals.

38
00:01:49,425 --> 00:01:53,009
Britannica notes that moving a MASH took 50 large trucks.

39
00:01:53,459 --> 00:01:56,740
But the war sent them so many wounded that some MASH

40
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:58,191
units grew to 200 beds.

41
00:01:58,641 --> 00:02:01,533
Casualties arrived by ambulance at a tent marked

42
00:02:01,533 --> 00:02:02,135
receiving.

43
00:02:02,585 --> 00:02:06,029
Inside, surgeons scrubbed in under bare bulbs, in

44
00:02:06,029 --> 00:02:06,943
canvas tents.

45
00:02:06,943 --> 00:02:10,317
Almost all of them were civilian doctors who had

46
00:02:10,317 --> 00:02:11,231
been drafted.

47
00:02:11,231 --> 00:02:14,535
By 1952, 90% of doctors in Korea were draftees.

48
00:02:14,985 --> 00:02:16,786
And Korea added something new:

49
00:02:16,786 --> 00:02:17,687
the helicopter.

50
00:02:17,687 --> 00:02:20,510
Helicopters carried more than 20,000 casualties

51
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:21,411
during the war.

52
00:02:21,861 --> 00:02:25,288
In 1951, 3 helicopter detachments were attached to

53
00:02:25,288 --> 00:02:26,042
MASH units.

54
00:02:26,042 --> 00:02:29,196
On one day that February, about 750 critically

55
00:02:29,196 --> 00:02:31,389
wounded soldiers were evacuated.

56
00:02:31,389 --> 00:02:34,885
The Eighth Army surgeon estimated that half of them

57
00:02:34,885 --> 00:02:38,656
would have died if only ground transport had been used.

58
00:02:39,106 --> 00:02:42,913
Defense Media Network reports that the death rate

59
00:02:42,913 --> 00:02:46,643
among seriously wounded soldiers fell from about

60
00:02:46,643 --> 00:02:50,684
4.5% in the Second World War to about 2.5% in Korea.

61
00:02:50,684 --> 00:02:54,724
Faster evacuation and medical advances are credited.

62
00:02:55,174 --> 00:02:58,983
One of those drafted surgeons, Richard Hornberger,

63
00:02:58,983 --> 00:03:00,963
served in the 8055th MASH.

64
00:03:00,963 --> 00:03:04,162
In 1968, he published a novel based on it.

65
00:03:04,162 --> 00:03:07,514
It became the film and the TV comedy M*A*S*H

66
00:03:08,014 --> 00:03:10,937
The MASH lasted far longer than the war that made it

67
00:03:10,937 --> 00:03:11,330
famous.

68
00:03:12,330 --> 00:03:16,038
Britannica notes MASH units were still in service in

69
00:03:16,038 --> 00:03:18,818
the Persian Gulf War, in 1990 and 1991.

70
00:03:19,268 --> 00:03:23,168
But during the Vietnam War, the Army began replacing

71
00:03:23,168 --> 00:03:26,994
its mobile surgical and evacuation hospitals with a

72
00:03:26,994 --> 00:03:27,819
new design:

73
00:03:27,819 --> 00:03:30,445
the combat support hospital, or CSH

74
00:03:30,895 --> 00:03:35,180
A full combat support hospital had 248 beds, split

75
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:36,809
into two companies.

76
00:03:36,809 --> 00:03:39,466
One with 164 beds, one with 84.

77
00:03:39,916 --> 00:03:42,567
The last MASH lasted until 2006.

78
00:03:42,567 --> 00:03:46,791
On October 16 that year, in Germany, the 212th MASH

79
00:03:46,791 --> 00:03:49,525
became a combat support hospital.

80
00:03:49,525 --> 00:03:52,921
Its last deployment had been to Pakistan.

81
00:03:53,371 --> 00:03:56,757
At the ceremony, its commander, Colonel Angel Lugo,

82
00:03:56,757 --> 00:03:58,549
made a promise to soldiers.

83
00:03:58,999 --> 00:04:02,966
Combat support hospitals were big, and the Army even

84
00:04:02,966 --> 00:04:05,407
kept whole hospitals in storage.

85
00:04:05,407 --> 00:04:09,374
In Japan, it stored and maintained the equipment for

86
00:04:09,374 --> 00:04:11,662
a 992 bed deployable hospital.

87
00:04:11,662 --> 00:04:15,248
In 2012, about 200 soldiers of the 325th Combat

88
00:04:15,248 --> 00:04:18,833
Support Hospital, from Missouri, set up medical

89
00:04:18,833 --> 00:04:20,969
tents there for an exercise.

90
00:04:21,419 --> 00:04:23,477
And then the Army changed the design again.

91
00:04:23,877 --> 00:04:27,503
Starting in 2017, the Army began turning its combat

92
00:04:27,503 --> 00:04:30,275
support hospitals into field hospitals.

93
00:04:31,275 --> 00:04:33,340
The new building block is small.

94
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:34,502
A 32-bed hospital.

95
00:04:34,502 --> 00:04:36,631
In Army terms, a Role 3 hospital:

96
00:04:36,631 --> 00:04:39,986
the level of care with surgeons, operating rooms and

97
00:04:39,986 --> 00:04:40,954
intensive care.

98
00:04:41,404 --> 00:04:42,288
Blocks stack.

99
00:04:42,288 --> 00:04:45,822
Add surgical and intensive care detachments, and the

100
00:04:45,822 --> 00:04:49,288
plan in the Army's own journal describes a hospital

101
00:04:49,288 --> 00:04:51,055
that can grow to 240 beds.

102
00:04:51,505 --> 00:04:52,226
Why change?

103
00:04:52,226 --> 00:04:55,571
The Army's own journal listed problems with the old

104
00:04:55,571 --> 00:04:56,030
design:

105
00:04:56,030 --> 00:04:59,048
how it was organized, its medical and surgical

106
00:04:59,048 --> 00:05:02,458
capabilities, and how hard it was to split it across

107
00:05:02,458 --> 00:05:03,770
different locations.

108
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:05,361
The point is speed.

109
00:05:05,361 --> 00:05:08,483
A small piece can go first, and the rest can follow.

110
00:05:08,933 --> 00:05:11,900
In the 10th Field Hospital, that first piece was an

111
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:14,052
early-entry element with just 6 beds.

112
00:05:14,052 --> 00:05:17,193
Four for patients, and two for damage-control surgery.

113
00:05:17,643 --> 00:05:19,820
That 2017 test was the proof.

114
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:23,123
The 627th Hospital Center and the 10th Field

115
00:05:23,123 --> 00:05:26,952
Hospital set up at Fort Polk and supported a 14-day

116
00:05:26,952 --> 00:05:27,628
exercise.

117
00:05:28,078 --> 00:05:31,676
Over 7 days, that training hospital received 242

118
00:05:31,676 --> 00:05:33,250
simulated casualties.

119
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:36,624
And in the last 3 days, the 6-bed early-entry

120
00:05:36,624 --> 00:05:40,223
element ran on its own, and treated more than 50

121
00:05:40,223 --> 00:05:42,697
simulated casualties in 72 hours.

122
00:05:43,147 --> 00:05:45,611
So what actually happens in those first hours?

123
00:05:46,611 --> 00:05:48,476
First, people and equipment arrive.

124
00:05:48,476 --> 00:05:50,394
By aircraft, by truck, by container.

125
00:05:50,844 --> 00:05:51,841
Then the site.

126
00:05:51,841 --> 00:05:55,400
Flat ground, or a building with a big empty floor.

127
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:55,970
A field.

128
00:05:55,970 --> 00:05:57,037
A sports arena.

129
00:05:57,037 --> 00:05:58,461
A convention center.

130
00:05:58,911 --> 00:06:00,285
Then the shelters go up.

131
00:06:00,285 --> 00:06:03,263
Soldiers unroll and stake down the tents, section by

132
00:06:03,263 --> 00:06:03,722
section.

133
00:06:04,172 --> 00:06:06,934
Hospital equipment comes out of its cases:

134
00:06:06,934 --> 00:06:08,578
beds, monitors, lab gear.

135
00:06:09,028 --> 00:06:10,670
Supplies have to come too.

136
00:06:10,670 --> 00:06:13,890
In Puerto Rico, soldiers trucked in food and water.

137
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:16,462
Then it's laid out like any hospital.

138
00:06:16,462 --> 00:06:19,158
Patients arrive at an emergency treatment area.

139
00:06:19,158 --> 00:06:21,969
Those who need surgery go to the operating rooms.

140
00:06:21,969 --> 00:06:23,690
After surgery, intensive care.

141
00:06:23,690 --> 00:06:26,615
Then intermediate care wards, until patients can be

142
00:06:26,615 --> 00:06:27,132
moved on.

143
00:06:27,582 --> 00:06:30,237
Behind that are the parts you rarely see:

144
00:06:30,237 --> 00:06:33,605
pharmacy, laboratory, dental, and medical logistics.

145
00:06:34,055 --> 00:06:37,492
In that 2017 design, the hospital's own vehicles

146
00:06:37,492 --> 00:06:40,643
could move 35% of its equipment in one trip.

147
00:06:40,643 --> 00:06:42,791
The rest needs more transport.

148
00:06:43,241 --> 00:06:45,581
And all of it has to be packed up again when the

149
00:06:45,581 --> 00:06:46,264
mission moves.

150
00:06:46,764 --> 00:06:48,870
Field hospitals aren't only for war.

151
00:06:49,870 --> 00:06:53,704
In October 2017, after Hurricane Maria, the 14th

152
00:06:53,704 --> 00:06:58,016
Combat Support Hospital arrived in Puerto Rico by air.

153
00:06:58,466 --> 00:07:01,171
It set up inside the damaged Humacao Arena.

154
00:07:01,171 --> 00:07:03,939
In less than a week, it had a full hospital:

155
00:07:03,939 --> 00:07:06,896
emergency services, surgery, two intensive care

156
00:07:06,896 --> 00:07:07,965
units, and x-ray.

157
00:07:08,415 --> 00:07:11,969
After three days of operations, it had seen 164

158
00:07:11,969 --> 00:07:14,615
patients and performed 6 surgeries.

159
00:07:15,065 --> 00:07:18,511
It was equipped to see about 100 patients a day,

160
00:07:18,511 --> 00:07:22,029
with 44 beds for patients needing intensive care.

161
00:07:22,029 --> 00:07:23,680
Running 24 hours a day.

162
00:07:24,130 --> 00:07:27,952
Then, in March 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic,

163
00:07:27,952 --> 00:07:31,621
Army hospital units deployed to American cities.

164
00:07:32,071 --> 00:07:35,522
In Seattle, the 627th Hospital Center set up 250

165
00:07:35,522 --> 00:07:38,902
beds inside the CenturyLink Field Event Center.

166
00:07:38,902 --> 00:07:42,353
With two emergency rooms, two operating rooms, a

167
00:07:42,353 --> 00:07:44,726
pharmacy and an x-ray department.

168
00:07:45,176 --> 00:07:47,912
In New York, soldiers helped turn the Javits

169
00:07:47,912 --> 00:07:50,400
Convention Center into a field hospital.

170
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:53,634
At first, it took patients without COVID-19, to take

171
00:07:53,634 --> 00:07:55,748
pressure off the city's hospitals.

172
00:07:56,198 --> 00:07:59,887
About 3,600 soldiers, sailors, airmen and marines

173
00:07:59,887 --> 00:08:03,049
deployed to New York and Washington state.

174
00:08:03,049 --> 00:08:06,737
Of the Seattle hospital, Lieutenant General Laura

175
00:08:06,737 --> 00:08:09,222
Richardson, the commander of U.S.

176
00:08:09,222 --> 00:08:12,609
Army North, said it's one of the most capable

177
00:08:12,609 --> 00:08:14,265
hospitals in the Army.

178
00:08:14,765 --> 00:08:17,637
Today, the Army is training field hospitals for a

179
00:08:17,637 --> 00:08:18,926
different kind of war.

180
00:08:19,926 --> 00:08:23,511
In February 2026, in Germany, the 512th Field

181
00:08:23,511 --> 00:08:28,132
Hospital set up a 32-bed hospital that could expand to 48.

182
00:08:28,132 --> 00:08:31,637
With two operating rooms and intensive care.

183
00:08:32,087 --> 00:08:33,768
208 soldiers took part.

184
00:08:33,768 --> 00:08:37,862
And the unit moved 100% of its own equipment, by itself.

185
00:08:38,312 --> 00:08:41,919
They also rehearsed long-distance casualty movement,

186
00:08:41,919 --> 00:08:45,526
by convoy and loading litters onto a train, all with

187
00:08:45,526 --> 00:08:46,982
simulated casualties.

188
00:08:46,982 --> 00:08:50,520
The commander of the 519th Hospital Center, Colonel

189
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:53,086
Crystal Belew, summed up the mission.

190
00:08:53,536 --> 00:08:56,840
A month later, at Fort McCoy, Wisconsin, the 11th

191
00:08:56,840 --> 00:09:00,345
Field Hospital from Fort Hood ran a 9-day collective

192
00:09:00,345 --> 00:09:03,244
training exercise, with medical simulation.

193
00:09:03,694 --> 00:09:07,272
One Army article argues the limit isn't clinical skill.

194
00:09:07,272 --> 00:09:07,922
It's beds.

195
00:09:08,372 --> 00:09:11,284
As one 2026 Army article puts it:

196
00:09:11,734 --> 00:09:13,803
The article describes three clocks.

197
00:09:13,803 --> 00:09:16,285
The emergency department works in minutes.

198
00:09:16,285 --> 00:09:17,940
The operating room in hours.

199
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:20,127
Intensive care and ward beds in days.

200
00:09:20,127 --> 00:09:23,082
If beds fill faster than patients can be moved on,

201
00:09:23,082 --> 00:09:26,038
the article warns, the hospital fails as a system.

202
00:09:26,488 --> 00:09:28,305
And drones change the picture.

203
00:09:28,305 --> 00:09:31,212
A field hospital spread across acres of tents is

204
00:09:31,212 --> 00:09:31,999
hard to hide.

205
00:09:32,450 --> 00:09:36,028
In March 2026, Army medics trained inside an old

206
00:09:36,028 --> 00:09:39,233
nuclear storage tunnel at Fort Hood, Texas.

207
00:09:39,233 --> 00:09:42,662
An officer told Stars and Stripes that precise

208
00:09:42,662 --> 00:09:46,166
targeting makes traditional field hospitals too

209
00:09:46,166 --> 00:09:46,986
vulnerable.

210
00:09:47,436 --> 00:09:50,231
The war between Russia and Ukraine, the article

211
00:09:50,231 --> 00:09:53,026
notes, has pushed people and troops into subway

212
00:09:53,026 --> 00:09:55,345
systems and parking garages for safety.

213
00:09:55,345 --> 00:09:58,318
So the Army is practicing hospitals that can hide.

214
00:09:58,918 --> 00:10:02,759
In 1951, a MASH was canvas tents, a sign by the

215
00:10:02,759 --> 00:10:05,292
road, and a line of ambulances.

216
00:10:05,742 --> 00:10:09,841
Today, it's a 32-bed module, designed to grow, in

217
00:10:09,841 --> 00:10:13,270
the Army's plan, into a 240-bed hospital.

218
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:15,440
The goal hasn't changed.

219
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:19,167
Bring surgery and intensive care close to the people

220
00:10:19,167 --> 00:10:20,458
who need it. Fast.

221
00:10:20,908 --> 00:10:22,119
Next on KEPT ALIVE:

222
00:10:22,119 --> 00:10:25,371
more stories about how medicine keeps people alive.

223
00:10:25,371 --> 00:10:27,476
Subscribe so you don't miss them.

224
00:10:28,276 --> 00:10:29,416
For education only.

225
00:10:29,416 --> 00:10:31,037
This is not medical advice.

226
00:10:31,037 --> 00:10:33,859
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
